<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Handling Missing Values                               -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with a real, verified story: Abraham Wald and the           -->
<!--  Statistical Research Group's WWII aircraft-survivability work.    -->
<!--  Chosen deliberately over a "disaster" story (unlike ch2/ch4):     -->
<!--  it is the classic case of reasoning correctly, and it is exactly  -->
<!--  about the imputation/deletion-adjacent question this chapter      -->
<!--  needs (why is data missing, and what does that imply about how    -->
<!--  to treat it), which none of the ch4 stories (inconsistency,       -->
<!--  duplication, silent recoding) actually covered.                  -->
<!--  Verified via multiple independent searches before writing:       -->
<!--    - Wald, A. (1943). "A Method of Estimating Plane Vulnerability  -->
<!--      Based on Damage of Survivors." Statistical Research Group,    -->
<!--      Columbia University, memorandum for the National Defense     -->
<!--      Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,    -->
<!--      1980).                                                       -->
<!--    - Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). "Abraham Wald's Work   -->
<!--      on Aircraft Survivability." Journal of the American           -->
<!--      Statistical Association, 79(386), 259-267. Peer-reviewed      -->
<!--      formalisation of Wald's original (classified at the time)     -->
<!--      memoranda - used as the authoritative secondary source for    -->
<!--      what his method actually did technically, specifically to    -->
<!--      avoid the oversimplified pop-science "just armour the         -->
<!--      engines" version. His actual method: assume hits are roughly  -->
<!--      uniformly distributed over the airframe by surface area, then -->
<!--      use returning-plane damage counts to infer conditional        -->
<!--      survival/vulnerability by section, explicitly correcting for  -->
<!--      the missing (non-returning) planes rather than reading        -->
<!--      returning-plane damage at face value.                        -->
<!--  MCAR/MAR/MNAR formal definitions and the na.rm/complete.cases     -->
<!--  material are adapted from the old, unbuilt drafts                -->
<!--  (ch6_descriptive_statistics.ptx sec-missing-data, and             -->
<!--  ch6_description_2.ptx subsec-missing-formal), rewritten to open   -->
<!--  with Wald as the MNAR example rather than presenting the taxonomy -->
<!--  cold.                                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-missing-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-missing-values">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> hit <c>NA</c> the moment it tried to
      compute a mean, and set the problem aside.
      <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> audited the gaps and started reasoning
      informally about why <c>bill_depth_mm</c>, <c>body_mass_g</c> and
      <c>sex</c> specifically had them.
      Both chapters promised a proper answer later.
      This is later.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central idea of this chapter is easy to state and easy to
      underestimate: <em>why</em> a value is missing determines what you are
      allowed to do about it.
      Sometimes missingness is background noise, safely ignorable.
      Sometimes it is the single most important piece of information in the
      dataset.
      The story below is the case where getting that distinction right won a
      real argument, in wartime, against everyone's first instinct.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Wald and the bombers                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-story">
    <title>Motivating introduction story</title>

    <p>
      todo Example in Kenya (based on its own abstract and how later papers
      describe it): Maina and colleagues at Kenya's Ministry of Health evaluated
      the completeness and quality of HIV-indicator data flowing through DHIS2 —
      the system where every health facility in the country submits monthly
      aggregate reports (on testing, treatment, PMTCT, etc.) that get rolled up
      to county and national totals.
      The paper is explicitly about the mechanics of assembling that rolled-up
      national picture from thousands of individual facility submissions over
      time, and it's one of the most-cited sources in the Kenyan literature on
      this exact problem — later papers describe it as the reference point for
      how to adjust for incomplete reporting, apart from where missing reports
      were considered an indication that no services were provided, which is a
      genuinely subtle data-shape judgment call: is a blank row a missing
      observation, or a true zero?
      Maina, I., et al.
      (2020).
      <em>Evaluating the quality and completeness of Kenya's routine HIV-indicator reporting data.</em>
      PLOS ONE, 15(11), e0243047.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="ss-ch8-stories">
    <title>todo</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        missing data stories: One MCAR, one MAR, and one MNAR.
        MNAR: The Bombers That Didn't Come Back Need a MCAR and MAR story.
        A famous good one.
        (Remove the challenger one.
        Unless that is MAR)
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wald-survivorship">
      <title>The Bombers That Didn't Come Back</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          During the Second World War, the US military examined bombers
          returning from missions over Germany and recorded where each one had
          been hit.
          The damage was not evenly spread.
          It clustered heavily on the wings and fuselage, far more than on the
          engines.
          The obvious recommendation followed immediately: reinforce the wings
          and fuselage, since that is clearly where the bullets go.
          TODO: Get image of the affected points on the planes.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-wald-the-catch">
        <title>The data was correct. The dataset was incomplete.</title>

        <p>
          The Statistical Research Group (SRG) at Columbia University, a team
          the US military had assembled to bring rigorous statistics to exactly
          these kinds of operational problems, included the mathematician
          Abraham Wald.
          Wald's objection to the obvious recommendation was not that the damage
          counts were wrong.
          They were almost certainly accurate.
          His objection was about which planes had been counted at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every bomber in that dataset had survived its mission and made it
          home.
          A bomber hit badly enough in the engine did not return to be
          inspected, photographed, and added to the tally.
          The data was not a random sample of <q>where bombers get hit.</q> It
          was a sample of <q>where bombers can be hit and still make it back</q>
          <mdash/> and those are very different questions.
          Heavy wing and fuselage damage was common in the surviving planes
          precisely <em>because</em> a plane could absorb a great deal of damage
          there and keep flying.
          The scarcity of engine damage in the data did not mean the engines
          were rarely hit.
          It meant a hit there was rarely survived, which is why those planes
          were missing from the dataset entirely.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-wald-the-method">
        <title>What Wald actually did about it</title>

        <p>
          Wald did not simply assert the intuition and stop there.
          Across a series of memoranda for the SRG, later formalised and
          republished by Mangel and Samaniego (1984), he built a statistical
          method for estimating vulnerability by aircraft section that
          explicitly accounted for the missing planes rather than reading the
          returning planes' damage at face value.
          Roughly: assuming hits were distributed over the airframe more or less
          in proportion to each section's surface area, the pattern of damage on
          <em>survivors</em> could be used to infer how survivable a hit to each
          section actually was <mdash/> and a section that rarely showed damage
          on returning planes, despite having no reason to be hit less often,
          was exactly the section a hit was least likely to be survived.
        </p>

        <p>
          The recommendation that followed was the reverse of the obvious one:
          reinforce the sections that were <em>under</em>-represented in the
          damage data, not over-represented, because that absence was the
          signal.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-wald-lesson">
          <title>The missing data was not noise. It was the finding.</title>

          <p>
            It would have been easy to treat the absent engine damage as nothing
            more than a smaller sample size for that section, safely ignorable,
            and analyse the rest of the data as if the missing rows simply were
            not there.
            Wald's insight was recognising that the missingness itself was
            informative: which planes were absent from the dataset depended
            directly on the thing being measured (survivability), not on some
            unrelated cause.
            Deleting the incomplete cases and moving on would not have lost a
            little statistical power.
            It would have inverted the conclusion.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Wald, A.
          (1943).
          <em>A Method of Estimating
        Plane Vulnerability Based on Damage of Survivors.</em>
          Statistical Research Group, Columbia University, memorandum for the
          National Defense Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval
          Analyses, 1980).
          Mangel, M.
          and Samaniego, F.J.
          (1984).
          <q>Abraham Wald's Work
        on Aircraft Survivability.</q>
          <em>Journal of the American
        Statistical Association</em>,
          79(386), 259 <ndash/> 267.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-challenger">
      <title>The Challenger disaster (1986)</title>

      <p>
        This final story is much more recent.
        And is not so much on multidimensionality.
        But hopefully shows the importance of showing information in graphics.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the morning of 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle <em>Challenger</em>
        stood on the launch pad at Kennedy Space Center in Florida.
        Mission STS-51-L was supposed to be a particularly important flight.
        Among the seven crew members was Christa McAuliffe, a 37-year-old
        teacher from New Hampshire, USA, who had been selected from more than
        11,000 applicants to become the first private citizen to travel into
        space.
        She was going to conduct lessons from orbit as part of NASA's "Teacher
        in Space" programme.
      </p>

      <p>
        The mission had attracted enormous public attention.
        Millions of schoolchildren were expected to watch the launch, and the
        shuttle programme was being presented as proof that space travel was
        becoming something ordinary people could participate in.
      </p>

      <p>
        The night before the launch, engineers at Morton Thiokol, the company
        that manufactured the shuttle's solid rocket boosters, were increasingly
        worried about the forecast temperature.
        Their concern centred on small rubber seals called <em>O-rings</em>.
        These seals were designed to prevent hot gases from escaping from the
        joints between sections of the solid rocket boosters.
      </p>

      <p>
        Engineers already knew that the O-rings had experienced damage on
        previous flights.
        They were particularly concerned that cold temperatures would make the
        rubber less flexible, slowing the O-rings' ability to seal the joint
        when the booster was ignited.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the coldest previous shuttle launch, STS-51-C in January 1985, the
        O-ring temperature had been about 53°F (12°C), and significant O-ring
        damage had been observed.
        The engineers therefore argued that they did not have evidence that the
        shuttle could safely launch at temperatures below this.
        They prepared charts and tables to explain their concerns during a
        telephone conference with NASA and Morton Thiokol managers.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-challenger-original-chart">
        <caption>
          One of the charts presented by Morton Thiokol engineers during the
          discussions about whether to launch Challenger.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/challenger-original-chart" width="80%"/>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-challenger-original-chart"/> contains information about
        previous O-ring problems, but the evidence is difficult to interpret.
        Rather than putting every previous shuttle flight into one simple
        comparison of temperature and O-ring damage, the information is split
        across tables and categories.
        Some of the most important comparisons are therefore difficult to see.
      </p>

      <p>
        The engineers' concerns were not accepted by NASA management.
        After a lengthy discussion, Morton Thiokol management eventually
        reversed its original recommendation and agreed that the launch could
        proceed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The next morning, Challenger launched.
      </p>

      <p>
        It was extremely cold.
        The ambient air temperature at the launch site was 36°F (2°C), around
        15°F colder than any previous shuttle launch.
      </p>

      <p>
        Just 73 seconds after lift-off, Challenger broke apart.
        All seven crew members were killed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The immediate physical cause of the disaster was the failure of an
        O-ring seal in one of the solid rocket boosters.
        Hot gases escaped through the failed joint and ultimately caused the
        catastrophic breakup of the shuttle.
        The Presidential Commission investigating the disaster concluded that
        the launch decision itself was seriously flawed, noting that the
        decision-makers were unaware of important evidence and of the engineers'
        continuing opposition to launching in the cold conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        But there is another question worth asking:
        <em>what did the data look like when the decision was being made?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Edward Tufte later examined the Challenger decision in
        <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997).
        His argument was that the problem was not simply that there was too
        little data.
        The engineers had accumulated years of information about O-ring
        performance.
        The problem was that the information was not displayed in a way that
        made the most important relationship obvious.
      </p>

      <p>
        Tufte reconstructed the evidence as a simple scatter plot.
        Each point represents a previous shuttle flight, with temperature on one
        axis and the amount of O-ring damage on the other.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-challenger-tufte">
        <caption>
          O-ring damage plotted against temperature for previous shuttle
          launches, with the forecast temperature for Challenger shown for
          comparison.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/challenger-tufte" width="80%"/>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Now the pattern is much easier to see.
        The colder launches tended to have greater O-ring damage.
        Indeed, every previous launch below 66°F had experienced some O-ring
        damage, while the most serious damage occurred on relatively cold
        flights.
        The planned Challenger launch temperature was far outside the range of
        previous shuttle experience.
      </p>

      <p>
        The important lesson is not that "a better graph would definitely have
        prevented the Challenger disaster." That would be too simple.
        The decision involved engineering uncertainty, management pressure,
        communication failures, organisational culture and a demanding launch
        schedule.
        Historians and researchers have also pointed out that Tufte's later
        reconstruction is not identical to the information actually available to
        engineers that night.
      </p>

      <p>
        The more useful lesson is about how we visualise multidimensional data.
        Data can be available without the pattern being visible.
        A table can contain the information needed to answer a question while
        simultaneously making that answer difficult to see.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the Challenger case, temperature, flight, O-ring damage and type of
        damage were all relevant.
        The challenge was to bring those dimensions together in a form that
        allowed the relationship between them to be seen.
        When the data are displayed appropriately, the question changes from
        <em>"Which flights had O-ring problems?"</em> to
        <em>"What happens to O-ring performance as temperature falls?"</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the power of visualising multidimensional data: sometimes the
        evidence is already there.
        The visualisation determines whether we can see it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Presidential Commission on the Space Shuttle
        Challenger Accident (1986).
        <em>Report to the President.</em> Washington, DC: US Government Printing
        Office.
      </p>

      <p>
        Tufte, E.R.
        (1997).
        <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and Narrative.</em>
        Cheshire, CT: Graphics Press.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-missing-applied">
    <title>Handling Missing Values, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to the gaps <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> found and reasoned
        about informally: 4 missing bill depths, 8 missing masses, 7 missing
        sexes.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        colSums(is.na(flamingos))

        #&gt;           lake        species bill_length_mm  bill_depth_mm  leg_length_mm
        #&gt;              0              0              0              4              0
        #&gt;    body_mass_g            sex           year
        #&gt;              8              7              0
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-flamingo-mechanism">
      <title>Which pattern applies to which gap</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> already sketched plausible
        mechanisms; now they can be named properly.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              A scale battery failing mid-morning, so every mass after that
              point goes unrecorded regardless of how heavy the bird was: this
              is <term>MCAR</term>-ish, provided nothing about which birds
              happened to be weighed that morning correlates with their mass.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Determining sex requiring closer handling, which the field team
              reserves for calmer birds: if
              <q>calm enough to handle
            closely</q> is itself
              unrelated to a bird's other recorded traits, this could be MCAR;
              if calmer birds are systematically a particular species or size
              class, which the dataset also records, it becomes <term>MAR</term>
              instead.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              If, on the other hand, the heaviest and most powerful birds are
              specifically the ones that struggle free before a mass reading can
              be taken, that is <term>MNAR</term>: the missingness of a mass
              value depends on the mass itself, the exact structure of Wald's
              problem, and it means the 336 recorded masses are not a fair
              sample of all 344 birds' true weights.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The dataset alone cannot tell you which of these is true.
        That has to come from knowing how the data was collected <mdash/> the
        same conclusion <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> reached, now with the
        vocabulary to say precisely what is at stake in each case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-na-rm-precise">
      <title>What <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> actually does</title>

      <p>
        It removes the missing observations and computes on what remains.
        Stated plainly:
        <em>you are no longer analysing the sample you
        thought you were.</em>
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405
      </program>
      <p>
        This is the mean of 336 birds, not 344, and it is essential to be clear
        that <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> is <em>not</em> imputation.
        It does not estimate the missing values.
        It excludes those birds from the calculation entirely, silently, unless
        you check <c>sum(!is.na(...))</c> yourself.
        If the missingness is MCAR, that exclusion costs you precision and
        nothing else.
        If it is MNAR, as with mass, it may cost you accuracy in a direction you
        cannot detect from the remaining 336 rows alone.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-complete-cases-applied">
      <title>Complete cases and listwise deletion</title>

      <program language="r">
        sum(complete.cases(flamingos))
        #&gt; [1] 325

        nrow(flamingos)
        #&gt; [1] 344
      </program>
      <p>
        Only 325 of 344 rows are complete: 19 rows, 5.5%, have at least one
        missing value somewhere, more than the 8 missing masses alone, because
        the gaps fall in different rows for different variables.
        Dropping every incomplete row ( <term>listwise
        deletion</term>
        <idx><h>listwise deletion</h></idx> ) is convenient and keeps every
        analysis on an identical sample, but it discards data unnecessarily: a
        bird missing only <c>sex</c> still has a perfectly good mass
        measurement.
        For descriptive work, prefer <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> variable by variable
        over blanket listwise deletion, and report the <m>n</m> for each.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-report-n-ch8">
        <title>Report the n</title>

        <p>
          Never report a mean, a standard deviation, or any other summary
          without also reporting the <m>n</m> it was computed from.
          A summary over 344 birds, 336, or 325 are different claims about
          different samples, and a table that omits <m>n</m> makes them
          indistinguishable.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-imputation-caution">
      <title>Imputation is a claim, not a fix</title>

      <p>
        A common third option, beyond na.rm-style exclusion and full listwise
        deletion, is <term>imputation</term> <idx><h>imputation</h></idx>:
        filling each missing value with an estimate, often the variable's mean
        or a model-based prediction, so no rows need to be dropped at all.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mass_imputed &lt;- ifelse(
          is.na(flamingos$body_mass_g),
          mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
          flamingos$body_mass_g
        )
      </program>
      <p>
        This line runs without complaint and produces a column with no missing
        values at all.
        It is also, if the missingness is MNAR, actively worse than doing
        nothing: it does not just fail to fix the bias, it hides it, replacing 8
        honestly-missing values with 8 confident-looking numbers built from the
        very mean that bias has already distorted.
        Imputation is a reasonable tool under MCAR or MAR, where the observed
        data genuinely contains the information needed to make a defensible
        estimate.
        Under MNAR it requires an external model of <em>why</em> the data is
        missing <mdash/> Wald's uniform-exposure assumption is exactly that kind
        of model <mdash/> not a one-line formula applied to whatever happens to
        remain.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <objectives xml:id="objectives-handling-missing-values">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain why the reason a value is missing determines what you may
          safely do about it.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Distinguish MCAR, MAR, and MNAR, and identify which applies to a given
          case.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Explain what <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> and <c>complete.cases()</c> actually
          do, and what they do not do.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Recognise when imputation is a reasonable tool and when it disguises
          rather than fixes a bias.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-missing-values">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-wald-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why deleting the aircraft with the heaviest
          hypothetical engine damage <mdash/> if the SRG had somehow had partial
          records for a few of them <mdash/> would still not have solved Wald's
          problem.
          What, specifically, does MNAR data require that listwise deletion
          cannot provide?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-classify-flamingo">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-flamingo-mechanism"/> gives three different possible
          mechanisms for the missing <c>body_mass_g</c> values, corresponding to
          MCAR, MAR, and MNAR.
          For each mechanism, describe one piece of additional information,
          beyond what <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> already contains, that would
          let you tell which mechanism actually applies.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-imputation-risk">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the code in <xref ref="ss-imputation-caution"/> as a starting
          point, explain what would happen to the mean and the standard
          deviation of <c>body_mass_g</c> if you used <c>mass_imputed</c>
          instead of the original column with <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>.
          Which one changes, which one doesn't, and why does that matter for
          anyone reading a report built on this column?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-crop-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why filtering the crop data to
          <c>sold == "yes"</c> before averaging ( ) produced a wrong answer even
          though the averaging itself was done correctly.
          Then describe one other variable, in any dataset you have worked with,
          where <q>missing</q> and <q>zero</q> would mean genuinely different
          things, and explain what filtering it out would silently assume.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-sex-mechanism">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Propose a plausible MNAR mechanism for the 7 missing <c>sex</c>
          values, distinct from the one given in
          <xref ref="ss-flamingo-mechanism"/>.
          Then propose a plausible MCAR mechanism for the same variable.
          What would you need to know about how the data was collected to decide
          between them?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>